---
import PrimerChat from "../PrimerChat.astro";
import { aiCardId, archiveAi } from "../../../lib/archive-ai";

const card = archiveAi.setup;
type Rec = { id: string; pages: number; approved: boolean };
const saved: Rec[] = [
  { id: "A", pages: 4, approved: true },
  { id: "B", pages: 3, approved: false },
  { id: "C", pages: 2, approved: true },
];
const changes = [
  { key: "a", label: "A grew to 5 pages", apply: (rs: Rec[]) => rs.map((r) => (r.id === "A" ? { ...r, pages: 5 } : r)) },
  { key: "b", label: "B was approved", apply: (rs: Rec[]) => rs.map((r) => (r.id === "B" ? { ...r, approved: true } : r)) },
  { key: "c", label: "C was deleted", apply: (rs: Rec[]) => rs.filter((r) => r.id !== "C") },
  { key: "d", label: "D appeared, 1 page, approved", apply: (rs: Rec[]) => [...rs, { id: "D", pages: 1, approved: true }] },
];
const oldCode = (rs: Rec[]) => rs.reduce((n, r) => n + r.pages, 0);
const fixedCode = (rs: Rec[]) => rs.reduce((n, r) => n + (r.approved ? r.pages : 0), 0);
const before = oldCode(saved);
const replay = fixedCode(saved);
const fromCode = replay - before;
const signed = (n: number) => (n === 0 ? "±0" : `${n < 0 ? "−" : "+"}${Math.abs(n)}`);
const pagesOf = (r: Rec) => `${r.pages} page${r.pages === 1 ? "" : "s"}`;

const delta = (c: (typeof changes)[number]) => fixedCode(c.apply(saved)) - replay;

const subsets = Array.from({ length: 1 << changes.length }, (_, m) => changes.filter((_, i) => m & (1 << i)));
const states = subsets.map((on) => {
  const rows = on.reduce((rs, c) => c.apply(rs), saved);
  const total = fixedCode(rows);
  const fromSource = total - replay;
  return {
    key: changes.map((c) => (on.includes(c) ? c.key : "")).join("") || "none",
    rows,
    total,
    diff: on.length === 0
      ? `${signed(total - before)} from yesterday, all of it from the fix`
      : `${signed(total - before)} from yesterday: ${signed(fromCode)} from the fix, ${on.map((c) => signed(delta(c))).join(" ")}${on.length > 1 ? ` = ${signed(fromSource)}` : ""} from the source`,
  };
});
const today = states[states.length - 1];
if (states.some((st) => st.total - replay !== changes.filter((c) => st.key.includes(c.key)).reduce((n, c) => n + delta(c), 0))) throw new Error("source changes are not additive");
const laneCss = states
  .map((st) => `.pa-rf${changes.map((c) => (st.key.includes(c.key) ? `:has([value="${c.key}"]:checked)` : `:not(:has([value="${c.key}"]:checked))`)).join("")} [data-s="${st.key}"]`)
  .join(",\n") + " { display: grid; }";

const checks = [
  ["Correcting yesterday", "Which input and which version of the code would you use to correct yesterday’s report? What total would you get?"],
  ["The same number twice", `The old report and the fresh report both say ${before}. Why does that tell you so little about whether the fix worked?`],
  ["Today and next week", "You need today’s report and want to be able to reproduce yesterday’s original report next week. What would you fetch, and what would you keep?"],
];
---

<PrimerChat
  prompt={card.prompt}
  module="Module 2"
  back={`../02-archive/#${aiCardId("setup")}`}
  about="This is a reply an AI assistant gave to Module 2's re-fetch study prompt, lightly edited. Send the same prompt yourself and you will get a different reply."
  turn="In a live chat, you would answer those three questions and the assistant would review your reasoning. To try that, copy the prompt into your own AI assistant. Then go back to Part 1 and name one change a site could have made since Friday that would move Saint Lambert's numbers."
>
  <h2 class="pr-title">Re-fetch or replay: fixing yesterday's report</h2>
  <p>Suppose you run a daily pipeline for a community archive. It fetches transcription records and reports how many pages have been approved. Yesterday you saved a copy of the records before running the pipeline.</p>
  <div class="pr-scroll">
    <table class="pr-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Record</th><th scope="col">Yesterday’s saved copy</th><th scope="col">Today’s live source</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {saved.map((r) => {
          const t = today.rows.find((x) => x.id === r.id);
          return <tr><th scope="row">{r.id}</th><td>{pagesOf(r)}, {r.approved ? "approved" : "pending"}</td><td>{t ? `${pagesOf(t)}, ${t.approved ? "approved" : "pending"}` : "Deleted"}</td></tr>;
        })}
        {today.rows.filter((t) => !saved.some((r) => r.id === t.id)).map((t) => <tr><th scope="row">{t.id}</th><td>Absent</td><td>{pagesOf(t)}, {t.approved ? "approved" : "pending"}</td></tr>)}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Yesterday’s code counted pending pages too, so it reported <strong>{before}</strong>. Today you fix the code to count only approved pages.</p>

  <h2>1. Replay yesterday’s copy</h2>
  <p>A <strong>replay</strong> runs the pipeline on the input you saved earlier. With yesterday’s copy and the fixed code, you get <strong>{replay}</strong> approved pages ({saved.filter((r) => r.approved).map((r) => `${r.pages} from ${r.id}`).join(" and ")}). Because the input stayed the same, the drop from {before} to {replay} shows the effect of your fix.</p>
  <p>You can also replay yesterday’s copy with the old code to reproduce the original <strong>{before}</strong>. Exact reproduction requires the same relevant settings, dependencies, and any other inputs the run used, such as the date if the code reads it.</p>

  <h2>2. Re-fetch today’s source</h2>
  <p>A <strong>re-fetch</strong> asks the source for a fresh copy. Since yesterday, one page count changed, a pending record was approved, a record was deleted, and a new record appeared. With the fixed code, today’s records give you <strong>{today.total}</strong> approved pages ({today.rows.filter((r) => r.approved).map((r) => `${r.pages} from ${r.id}`).join(", ").replace(/, ([^,]*)$/, ", and $1")}).</p>
  <p class="pr-pull">Yesterday’s report and today’s fresh report both say {before}. That equality hides two changes.</p>
  <p>The code fix brings yesterday’s total down from {before} to {replay}, and the source updates bring the corrected total back up from {replay} to {today.total}.</p>
  <figure class="pr-card pa-rf">
    <fieldset class="pa-rf-controls">
      <legend>What changed at the source since yesterday</legend>
      {changes.map((c) => <label><input type="checkbox" value={c.key} checked /> <span>{c.label}</span></label>)}
    </fieldset>
    <div class="pa-lanes">
      <div class="pa-lane is-old">
        <p class="pa-lane-head"><b>Yesterday's report</b><span>saved copy, old code</span></p>
        <ul>{saved.map((r) => <li><span>{r.id} · {r.approved ? "approved" : "pending"}</span><b>{r.pages}</b></li>)}</ul>
        <p class="pa-lane-total"><span>Pages counted</span><b>{before}</b></p>
      </div>
      <div class="pa-lane is-replay">
        <p class="pa-lane-head"><b>Replay</b><span>saved copy, fixed code</span></p>
        <ul>{saved.map((r) => <li><span>{r.id} · {r.approved ? "approved" : "pending"}</span><b>{r.approved ? r.pages : 0}</b></li>)}</ul>
        <p class="pa-lane-total"><span>Pages counted</span><b>{replay}</b></p>
        <p class="pa-lane-diff">{signed(fromCode)} from yesterday, all of it from the fix</p>
      </div>
      {states.map((st) => (
        <div class="pa-lane is-refetch" data-s={st.key}>
          <p class="pa-lane-head"><b>Re-fetch</b><span>today's source, fixed code</span></p>
          <ul>{st.rows.map((r) => <li><span>{r.id} · {r.approved ? "approved" : "pending"}</span><b>{r.approved ? r.pages : 0}</b></li>)}</ul>
          <p class="pa-lane-total"><span>Pages counted</span><b>{st.total}</b></p>
          <p class="pa-lane-diff">{st.diff}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <figcaption>Switch the source's changes off and on. The replay never moves, because its input is the saved copy. The re-fetch moves with the source, and its total mixes the fix with whatever changed.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>3. What a re-fetch overwrites</h2>
  <p>If you save the re-fetch over your only copy of the source, you replace yesterday’s snapshot with today’s. You lose the old page count for A, B’s pending status, and C’s record from that saved copy. You’d need another copy or source history to replay yesterday’s input.</p>

  <h2>4. When to re-fetch</h2>
  <p>A re-fetch is the right step when you need today’s report, when the source has corrected its own data, or when your saved copy is incomplete. Treat it as a separate step so you can measure what the code fix changed on yesterday’s input, then see what the source updates changed with the fixed code.</p>
  <aside class="pr-course" aria-label="Course note">
    <p class="pr-label">Course note</p>
    <p>Kick's plan in Part 1 is the reply's re-fetch: fix the tidy rule, fetch every school's site again, regenerate. Riley's ask is the replay: the fixed rule over the pages saved on Friday, with no site asked again.</p>
    <p>Touchline keeps the latest capture of each page, the reply's “only copy”, so Kick's re-fetch would also have overwritten the pages Friday's table was built from. The reply can print a table of what changed at its source. A re-fetch hands back new pages, not a list of changes, and nothing in Part 1 shows what the sites published after Friday. That is why its prediction's honest answer is “can't tell”.</p>
  </aside>

  <h2>5. Check your understanding</h2>
  <ol class="pr-checks">{checks.map(([name, q]) => <li><b>{name}</b><span>{q}</span></li>)}</ol>
</PrimerChat>

<Fragment set:html={`<style>${laneCss}</style>`} />

<style>
  .pa-rf-controls { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.4rem 1.2rem; margin: 0 0 0.7rem; padding: 0; border: 0; font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .pa-rf-controls legend { width: 100%; margin-bottom: 0.3rem; color: var(--tl-muted); font-size: 0.78rem; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.06em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .pa-rf-controls label { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.4rem; min-height: 40px; cursor: pointer; }
  .pa-rf-controls input { width: 1.1rem; height: 1.1rem; accent-color: var(--pr-ai); }
  .pa-lanes { display: grid; gap: 0.6rem; }
  @media (min-width: 46rem) { .pa-lanes { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); } }
  .pa-lane { display: grid; align-content: start; padding: 0.65rem 0.8rem; border: 1px solid var(--tl-line); border-top-width: 4px; border-radius: 8px; background: var(--tl-bg); }
  .pa-lane.is-old { border-top-color: var(--tl-muted); }
  .pa-lane.is-replay { border-top-color: var(--pr-green); }
  .pa-lane.is-refetch { display: none; border-top-color: #d9822b; }
  :global(.pr) .pa-lane-head { display: grid; margin: 0 0 0.4rem; }
  .pa-lane-head b { color: var(--sl-color-white); }
  .pa-lane-head span { color: var(--tl-muted); font-size: 0.82rem; }
  .pa-lane ul { display: grid; gap: 0.2rem; margin: 0 0 0.4rem; padding: 0; list-style: none; font-size: 0.88rem; }
  .pa-lane li { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 0.5rem; }
  .pa-lane li em { color: #d9822b; font-style: normal; font-weight: 600; }
  .pa-lane li b { font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  :global(.pr) .pa-lane-total { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin: 0; padding-top: 0.35rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--tl-line); }
  .pa-lane-total b { color: var(--sl-color-white); font-size: 1.3rem; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  :global(.pr) .pa-lane-diff { margin: 0.35rem 0 0; color: var(--tl-muted); font-size: 0.82rem; }
</style>
